# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets. This repo is intended to go public. Nothing in this section may ever
# be committed. If any of it lands in a commit, treat the credential as
# compromised and rotate it -- git history is forever and public forks are
# instant. See docs/adr/0002-public-repo-secret-hygiene.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p8
*.pkcs8
*.pfx
*.p12
secrets/
.secrets/

# Fly.io may write local state that references app names and tokens
.fly/
fly.*.local.toml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data. Operational database, recorded market data, and the Parquet lake. All
# regenerable, all large, none of it belongs in git.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
data/
warehouse/target/
warehouse/dbt_packages/
warehouse/logs/
*.duckdb
*.duckdb.wal

# dbt writes a machine-local anonymous telemetry id here. Regenerated on demand;
# committing it just makes every machine dirty the working tree on first run.
warehouse/.user.yml

# Parquet lake. tests/fixtures/ is explicitly NOT ignored -- captured API
# payloads are the wire-format contract and must be committed.
*.parquet
!tests/fixtures/**

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
.venv/
venv/
env/
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node / Next.js
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
.next/
out/
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo
.vercel

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors and OS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Claude Code local state. The committed .claude/ content (skills, agents) is
# deliberate and travels with the repo; only machine-local settings are ignored.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.claude/settings.local.json

# Agent worktrees (ADR 0003). Separate checkouts, never content of this one.
.claude/worktrees/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scratch output from screenshot and probe runs. `.tmp_shots390/` -- six PNGs,
# 1.5MB -- was committed by accident in a92ac42 and found by the pre-public
# history audit on 2026-08-10. The images were demo data, so nothing had to be
# rewritten, but a screenshot of this UI is one live run away from showing a
# real position or bankroll. The glob, not the one directory name, is the
# guard: the suffix is generated per run, so `.tmp_shots391/` would otherwise
# be addable tomorrow. One pattern, not two -- `.tmp_*` already matches
# directories, so a separate `.tmp_*/` line never fires and would be decoration.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.tmp_*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# curl cookie jars. `start.md`'s phone check authenticates against the LIVE
# instance and writes its session cookie to a file, and every runbook that does
# so has told the reader to delete it afterwards -- an instruction, not a guard,
# on a public repo where every push publishes immediately.
#
# A session cookie for the money instance is a credential in every sense that
# matters: it is bearer-shaped, it is accepted by `/api/orders`, and unlike the
# Kalshi key it is *generated during ordinary debugging*, so it is created far
# more often than the things `*.pem` and `.env` cover.
#
# Globbed rather than named. `jar.txt` is what the runbooks happen to use today;
# the next reader writes `cookies.txt` and the specific name protects nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
jar.txt
*.cookies
cookies.txt
cookiejar*

# transient log captures
live.log
